[Event "Lasker endgames: Take the opposition and go around"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/2k5/8/8/3K4/2P5/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/H17vt8vC"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/2k5/8/8/3K4/2P5/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E01"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "Direct opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 53"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Lasker endgames: learn the opposition. Move your king to c4 and make his king answer first. [%cal Yd3c4] } 1. Kc4 { Opposition: your kings face each other on c4 and c6, one square apart, and whoever has to move must give way. It is his move now. [%cal Gd3c4] } (1. c4 { That only draws: the pawn spends its one spare step right away, so nothing is left in reserve to hand him the move later. } 1... Kb7 2. Ke4 2... Kc7 { His king reaches c7 and blocks the pawn's road for good, and the reserve tempo you spent on move one is exactly what you needed here. Drawn. [%cal Rb7c7] }) (1. Kd4 { Also a win: you skip the opposition for now, but the pawn's spare step still lets you force it later. } 1... Kd6 { He takes the opposition back on d6, but the spare pawn step is still yours to spend and hand it straight back to him. [%cal Bc6d6] }) 1... Kd6 { Opposition forces him off c6. Move your king to b5 and slip past him on the wing he just left. [%cal Yc4b5] } 2. Kb5 { You outflank him on the queenside: your king now stands ahead of his, clearing the road for the pawn toward the sixth row. [%cal Gc4b5] } (2. Kb3 { Also a win: the reserve pawn step is still unplayed, so a less direct king move here does not cost the game. } 2... Kc7 { He hurries back to guard the queening road on c7, and the spare pawn step is exactly what will force him off it again. [%cal Bd6c7] }) (2. Kb4 { Also a win, one row further back than the main path: the spare pawn step still lets you win the waiting game whenever you choose. } 2... Kc7 { He hurries back to block the pawn's road, but you are still ahead of him and the spare step is still yours to spend. [%cal Bd6c7] }) (2. Kc3 { This retreat gives the win straight back: stepping off c4 lets him meet your king move for move and take the opposition himself. } 2... Kc5 3. Kb3 3... Kb5 { His king takes back the opposition on b5, and the ground your king fought for on the queenside is gone. [%cal Rc5b5] }) (2. Kd4 { Also a win: this route keeps you ahead of the pawn, and the spare pawn step is still there to force the issue later. } 2... Ke7 { He shifts across to e7, but your king still leads the way around him and the spare pawn step is untouched. [%cal Bd6e7] }) 2... Kc7 { He hurries back to block the pawn's road. Do not chase his king: pass him the move instead with one quiet step, c2 to c3. [%cal Yc2c3] } 3. c3 { One short step is all you spend, and the long step still waits in reserve. Now it is his turn to move. [%cal Gc2c3] } (3. c4 { Both squares at once, and the pawn runs out ahead of your king with nobody left to shield it. } 3... Kb7 4. c5 4... Kc7 5. c6 5... Kc8 { His king shuffles between c7 and c8 in front of the pawn, and it never queens. Drawn. [%csl Rc8] }) (3. Kc5 { This wins too: your king faces his straight on instead of touching the pawn at all. } 3... Kb7 { Winning as well, and the quiet pawn step is still sitting in your pocket for later. [%cal Bc7b7] }) (3. Kb4 { Also winning, though dropping a row here costs you a move for nothing. } 3... Kb6 { Still winning, but your king has to retrace its steps back to b5 before the outflank can continue. [%cal Bc7b6]}) (3. Kc4 { Winning as well, but stepping back beside the pawn means walking your king forward all over again. } 3... Kc6 { You still win, and now you have to walk the king forward again from where it started. [%cal Bc7c6] }) (3. Ka5 { This wins too, though sliding the king out to the edge on a5 leaves less room for error. } 3... Kc6 { Winning, but from a5 only one square keeps it going, so the road ahead is narrow. [%cal Bc7c6] }) (3. Ka4 { Also winning, although the edge of the board is a strange place to send a king that could stay central. } 3... Kc6 { The win holds, and your king now faces a long walk back toward the middle before it can help. [%cal Bc7c6] }) 3... Kd6 { He crosses over to d6. Now the pawn can take its long step: push it to c4. [%cal Yc3c4] } 4. c4 { Now the pawn takes its long step, with your king already up on b5 to shield its road. [%cal Gc3c4] } (4. Kc4 { Your king steps back onto c4, right next to the pawn, and gives up every square the outflanking march had gained. } 4... Kc6 5. Kb3 5... Kb5 { His king faces yours with one square between them, and it is your move. The win is gone. [%csl Rb5] }) (4. Kb6 { Also winning: climbing to b6 keeps the road to the pawn open even though it costs a move. } 4... Kd5 { Winning too: the pawn still has its long step in hand, and your king is still in front of it. [%cal Bd6d5] }) (4. Kb4 { Also winning, but dropping to b4 means the pawn has to wait one more turn before its long step. } 4... Kc6 { The win is still there, though from b4 only one square keeps it alive. [%cal Bd6c6] }) 4... Kc7 { He blocks the road on c7. Step in front of the pawn yourself: king to c5. [%cal Yb5c5] } 5. Kc5 { Your kings stare at each other from c5 and c7, one square apart, and he has to move. Now watch the road open. [%cal Gb5c5][%tail] } (5. c5 { The pawn charges off on its own, which lets his king get in front of it for good. } 5... Kb7 6. c6+ 6... Kc7 { His king sits in front of the pawn on c7, and your king can never force it aside. Drawn. [%csl Rc7] }) (5. Kb4 { Backwards: stepping away from the pawn hands back all the ground your king just won. } 5... Kb6 6. c5+ 6... Kc6 { His king plants itself on c6 in front of the pawn, and there it stays for good. Drawn. [%csl Rc6] }) 5... Kb7 6. Kd6 6... Kb8 7. Kd7 7... Ka7 8. c5 8... Ka6 9. c6 9... Kb5 10. c7 10... Kc4 11. c8=Q+ { A new queen on c8, with check, and his king is stranded on c4. The opposition won the road, and your king walked the pawn straight up it. [%csl Gc8] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Choose a wing after taking opposition"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/4k3/2p3p1/2P3P1/4K3/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/mlOgRUIi"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/4k3/2p3p1/2P3P1/4K3/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E02"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Direct opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 60"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Both pawn pairs are locked and nothing can capture yet. The whole game turns on whether your king can force his to step aside first. } 1. Ke4 { You take the opposition on e4. He must give way, and one king cannot watch both wings of pawns at once. [%cal Ge3e4] } (1. Kd3 { That only draws: from d3 you are not in opposition, so he simply mirrors your king and both wings stay guarded. } 1... Kf6 2. Ke2 2... Ke7 { The win is gone: the pawns stay locked, and his king holds every entry square. [%cal Rf6e7] }) (1. Kf3 { That only draws too: f3 gives up the opposition the same way, and his king answers you move for move. } 1... Ke5 2. Ke3 2... Kf6 { This leaves the pawns locked. His king guards the entry squares, and the win is gone. [%cal Re5f6] }) 1... Kd6 { He commits his king to the queenside wing, and that leaves the kingside pawn on g5 with nobody to watch it. } 2. Kf5 { You walk in on the other wing, and his pawn on g5 has nobody left to guard it. Now watch. [%cal Ge4f5][%tail] } (2. Kd3 { This only draws: d3 abandons the wing you just won access to, and his king dashes straight back to cover it. } 2... Kd7 3. Kc3 3... Kc6 { The win slips away: with the pawns still locked, his king covers the squares you needed. [%cal Rd7c6] }) 2... Kd7 3. Kxg5 3... Ke6 4. Kg6 4... Ke5 5. g5 5... Ke6 6. Kh6 6... Ke5 7. g6 7... Kd4 8. g7 8... Kxc4 9. g8=Q+ { A new queen on g8, with check. His king spent the whole game chasing your c pawn and never got back to the kingside. [%csl Gg8] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Bring the king back before pushing"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/6k1/8/7K/8/2P5/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/A2t5HfyC"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "6k1/8/7K/8/2P5/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E03"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Direct opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 51"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Your king is far from its own pawn on c4. Can you bring it home while still keeping his king off the sixth rank? } 1. Kg6 { Your king turns back toward its own pawn first: push the pawn now and his king simply catches it before yours arrives. } (1. c5 { That only draws: the pawn runs ahead alone, and his king reaches it long before yours can escort it home. } 1... Kf7 2. Kg5 2... Ke6 { His king reaches e6 right in front of the pawn's road, and yours is still three squares from home. [%cal Rf7e6] }) (1. Kg5 { That only draws too: g5 wastes a move sideways while his king slips straight toward the pawn's file. } 1... Kg7 2. Kf5 2... Kf7 { The win is gone: his king holds firm, and the sixth-rank technique was the only road through. [%cal Rg7f7] }) 1... Kf8 { Black's king clings to the back rank, hoping to slip past once your own king commits to the pawn. } 2. Kf6 { Your king on f6 blocks every square his own king needs, so the pawn can sit safely wherever it is. } (2. c5 { That only draws: pushing the pawn now lets his king slip in behind you instead of staying shut out. } 2... Ke7 3. Kf5 3... Kd7 { This loses the win: his king reaches d7, the very square the sixth-rank escort was meant to keep him off. [%cal Re7d7] }) (2. Kf5 { That only draws: f5 drops off the sixth rank a move too soon, and his king slips back in behind you. } 2... Kf7 3. c5 3... Ke7 { The win slips away here: his king reclaims e7, and the escort needed one more move to shut him out for good. [%cal Rf7e7] }) 2... Ke8 { He gives way again. Can you keep your king on the sixth rank? } 3. Ke6 { Your king is back on e6, opposite his king on e8 with one square between: opposition, and he has to give way. [%tail] } (3. c5 { This only draws: the pawn runs before your king has sealed him out, and his king slips past behind it. } 3... Kd7 4. Ke5 4... Kc6 { His king reaches c6 and holds the position for good: the win is gone. [%cal Rd7c6] }) (3. Ke5 { That only draws: e5 steps off the sixth rank, and his king darts straight back in behind you. } 3... Ke7 4. Kd5 4... Kd7 { The win is gone: his king keeps its grip, exactly what the sixth-rank technique was meant to prevent. [%cal Re7d7] }) 3... Kd8 4. Kd6 4... Ke8 5. c5 5... Kf7 6. c6 6... Kf6 7. c7 7... Kf5 8. c8=Q+ { Queen, with check! Your king escorted the pawn all the way home, exactly as the sixth-rank technique promised. [%csl Gc8] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Choose the correct retreat"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/3k4/3P4/4K3/8/8/8/8_b_-_-_0_3"]
[White "Opponent"]
[Black "Student"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/7IyMYzDT"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/3k4/3P4/4K3/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 3"]
[ChapterId "E04"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Direct opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 50"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]

{ You are a pawn down: hold the draw. His pawn on d6 is close to queening, so which retreat keeps its promotion square covered? } 3... Kd8 { You step straight back toward the pawn's own file, saving the right opposition. [%cal Gd7d8] } (3... Kc8 { That loses: stepping to c8 abandons the file the pawn queens on, and his king simply walks in behind it. } 4. Ke6 4... Kd8 5. d7 5... Kc7 6. Ke7 { This retreat loses the opposition, and White walks the pawn in for promotion. [%cal Re6e7] }) (3... Ke8 { That loses: e8 looks close to the correct square, but it is one file off, and his king finds the gap. } 4. Ke6 4... Kd8 5. d7 5... Kc7 6. Ke7 { The wrong square costs you the opposition. White escorts the pawn home from here. [%cal Re6e7] }) 4. Ke6 { White's king marches up to e6, ready to shoulder yours off the queening square. } 4... Ke8 { You take the opposition now, not one move too early. [%cal Gd8e8] } (4... Kc8 { This loses the draw too: c8 gives up the opposition again, and now his king is already close enough to punish it. } 5. Ke7 5... Kb7 6. d7 { Losing the opposition here lets White's king shepherd the pawn to promotion. [%cal Rd6d7] }) 5. d7+ { The pawn steps to d7 with check: one square from queening, and your king must answer right now. } 5... Kd8 { Your king is back in front of the pawn on d8. If his king steps to d6 that is stalemate, so watch him leave the pawn instead. [%cal Ge8d8][%tail] } (5... Kf8 { That loses: you leave the promotion square unguarded. } 6. Kf6 6... Kg8 7. Kg6 7... Kf8 8. d8=Q# { Mate. The wrong retreat let his king escort the pawn to promotion. [%cal Rd7d8] }) 6. Kd5 6... Kxd7 { Your king collects the pawn on d7. Two bare kings and nothing else on the board: the draw is yours. [%csl Gd7] } 0-1


[Event "Lasker endgames: Take opposition from far away"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4k3/8/8/1p5p/1P5P/8/3K4/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/4dGrXtVG"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "4k3/8/8/1p5p/1P5P/8/3K4/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E05"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "Distant opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 62"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Distant opposition works the same way, just from far off. Move your king to e2, matching his king's file. [%cal Yd2e2] } 1. Ke2 { Distant opposition: your kings share the e column, with an odd number of squares between them, so the rule still holds. [%cal Gd2e2][%csl Ge2,Ge8] } (1. Kd3 { Kd3 leaves the e column, and now an even number of squares sits between the kings: no more distant opposition. } 1... Kd7 2. Ke3 2... Ke7 { The win is gone: he takes the opposition and blocks your path to the pawns. [%cal Rd7e7] }) (1. Ke3 { That also draws: e3 keeps the e column, but now an even count sits between you, so he takes the opposition instead. } 1... Ke7 2. Kf3 2... Kf7 { This hands him the opposition, and with it the win. He blocks your way to the pawns. [%cal Re7f7] }) 1... Kd7 { He steps toward the center. Move your king to d3 to keep the opposition. [%cal Ye2d3] } 2. Kd3 { You meet him on the d column, the gap between the kings still odd: the opposition holds. [%cal Ge2d3] } (2. Ke3 { This only draws: e3 breaks the file you shared with him, and he matches you across to keep the count even. } 2... Ke7 3. Kf3 3... Kf7 { The win is gone here. His king takes the opposition and shuts you out of the pawns. [%cal Re7f7] }) 2... Ke7 { He changes files. Move your king to e3 and keep him facing you. [%cal Yd3e3] } 3. Ke3 { You follow him across to the e column, the gap still odd: the opposition holds. [%cal Gd3e3] } (3. Kd4 { That draws too: stepping to d4 lets him match you file for file and keep the count even. } 3... Kd6 4. Ke4 4... Ke6 { He gets the opposition this way, and the win is gone with it. [%cal Rd6e6] }) 3... Kd6 { He steps closer. Move your king to d4 and take direct opposition. [%cal Ye3d4] } 4. Kd4 { The opposition is now direct. His king must give way to one wing. [%cal Ge3d4] } (4. Ke4 { That draws too: e4 gives up the direct opposition you just reached, and he steps straight back in front of you. } 4... Ke6 5. Kd4 5... Kd6 { The win slips away: his king holds the opposition and bars your path to the pawns. [%cal Re6d6] }) 4... Kc6 { He gives way to the queen side. Now take the road he left open: king to e5. [%cal Yd4e5] } 5. Ke5 { Your king slips past his and sets off for the h pawn. [%cal Gd4e5] } (5. Kd3 { That does not just lose the win, it loses the game: stepping back to d3 lets his king through to e4, and your h pawn falls next. } 5... Kd5 6. Kc3 6... Ke4 { His king is through on e4 and your h pawn falls next. You are the one in trouble. [%csl Re4] }) (5. Ke3 { A step backwards, and the road you just opened closes again. } 5... Kd5 6. Kd3 6... Ke5 { He answers every step you take and your king never gets across. Drawn. [%csl Re5] }) (5. Ke4 { This wins too: one row lower still gets your king across in time, since his own king is too far away to stop it. } 5... Kd6 6. Kd4 { Winning still, but from e4 only d4 holds him, so the road is a single square wide. [%cal Be4d4] }) 5... Kc7 { He drops back to c7. Keep walking: king to f5. [%cal Ye5f5] } 6. Kf5 { Your king is halfway across while his stays home minding the b pawn. [%cal Ge5f5] } (6. Kd4 { Back toward the b pawn, and the road you opened closes behind you. } 6... Kd6 7. Ke4 7... Ke6 { His king faces yours on e6 and you never get round to the h pawn. Drawn. [%csl Re6] }) (6. Kf4 { Also winning: f4 is a row lower than the march through f5, but your king still beats his back to the queenside. } 6... Kd6 7. Kg5 { Winning too, though from f4 only g5 carries on, so there is no room for error. [%cal Bf4g5] }) (6. Kf6 { Also winning: the high road through f6 gets there just as fast, since his king never left the queenside. } 6... Kd6 7. Kg5 { Winning as well, and here both g5 and g6 keep the march going. [%cal Bf6g5] }) (6. Kd5 { Winning too, though turning back toward the b pawn wastes the ground your march had just gained. } 6... Kb6 7. Kd6 { Still winning, though those two moves toward b6 and back gained nothing at all. [%cal Bd5d6] }) (6. Ke6 { This wins as well: staying on the e column one more move still gets you across before his king can react. } 6... Kc6 7. Ke5 { The win holds, but only e5 keeps him shut out from here. [%cal Be6e5] }) (6. Ke4 { Also winning: dropping to e4 looks like a wasted row, but his king is still too far off to punish it. } 6... Kd6 7. Kd4 { You still win, and your king is back on the square it started from. [%cal Be4d4] }) 6... Kd6 { He hurries after you. One more step: king to g5. [%cal Yf5g5] } 7. Kg5 { From g5 your king leans on the h pawn, and his king is far too late. [%cal Gf5g5] } (7. Kf6 { One row too high, and it lets his king back into the middle. } 7... Kd5 8. Kf5 8... Kc4 { His king reaches c4 and now your b pawn is the one in danger. Drawn. [%csl Rc4] }) (7. Kg6 { This wins too: one row higher still leans on the h pawn in time, since his king is still stuck on the queenside. } 7... Ke5 { Winning as well: the pawn on h5 is still there for the taking. [%cal Bg6h5] }) 7... Ke5 { He arrives far too late. Collect the pawn: king takes on h5. [%cal Yg5h5] } 8. Kxh5 { His h pawn is gone. You are a pawn up, your king owns h5, and the distant opposition paid for every step of that walk. [%csl Gh5] } (8. Kg6 { Also winning: circling through g6 leaves the pawn alive one extra move, but nothing else changes. } 8... Ke4 { Winning too: nothing has changed except that you spent a move on nothing. [%cal Bg6h5] }) (8. Kh6 { Also winning: collecting from h6 instead just costs one extra move, since the pawn has nowhere to run. } 8... Ke4 9. Kxh5 { The pawn falls anyway; you simply arrived a move behind. [%cal Bh6h5] }) 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Do not rush toward the pawn"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/8/k1p5/2P1K2p/7P/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/qYdJRKeU"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/8/k1p5/2P1K2p/7P/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E06"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Distant opposition"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 67"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Rushing at his pawn can lose yours. Can you take distant opposition first? } 1. Ke5 { You take distant opposition. His king must yield a way toward the c5 pawn. [%tail] [%cal Ge4e5] } (1. Kd5 { That loses: d5 heads straight for his pawn but leaves c4 undefended, and his king reaches it first. } 1... Kb4 2. Ke4 2... Kxc4 { Your c pawn falls, and Black wins the pawn race that follows. [%cal Rb4c4] }) (1. Kf5 { That loses: f5 chases the h pawn right away, but leaves c4 undefended and his king gets there first. } 1... Kb4 2. Kg5 2... Kxc4 3. Kxh4 { This drops the c pawn, and Black wins the race that is left. [%cal Rg5h4] }) 1... Ka6 2. Kd5 2... Kb6 3. Kd6 3... Kb7 4. Kxc5 { His c pawn is yours: you are a pawn up, your king on c5 is the active one, and his h pawn is jammed against yours on h3. [%cal Gd6c5] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Save a pawn step to gain a turn"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/3k4/1p6/1P1K4/8/2P5/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/ghkBBhiX"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/3k4/1p6/1P1K4/8/2P5/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E07"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "Reserve tempi"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 57"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ A reserve tempo is a spare pawn step. Push your c pawn just one square, to c3. [%cal Yc2c3] } 1. c3 { You spend one pawn step and make his king move first. [%cal Gc2c3] } (1. c4 { That gives away the win: both pawn steps at once trades off the reserve tempo, and this pawn ending with none left over is simply drawn. } 1... bxc4 2. Kxc4 2... Kc6 { The exchanges give him a drawn king-and-pawn ending. Keep one step in reserve. [%cal Rd6c6] }) (1. Kc3 { Also a win: the reserve pawn move is still unplayed, so a king move here costs you nothing. } 1... Ke5 { Also winning. The spare pawn move is still sitting in reserve for later. [%cal Bd6e5] }) (1. Kd3 { Also a win: d3 spends no tempo either, so the reserve pawn step is still there whenever you need it. } 1... Ke5 { This wins too, and the spare pawn move is still available whenever you need it. [%cal Bd6e5] }) (1. Ke3 { Also a win: e3 leaves the reserve pawn move untouched too, so nothing about the result changes. } 1... Ke5 { Still winning: you have not spent the reserve pawn move yet. [%cal Bd6e5] }) (1. Ke4 { Also a win: heading to e4 does not spend the reserve pawn step either, so it is still there when you need it. } 1... Ke6 { You can spend the spare move later. This line just uses it right away. [%cal Bd6e6] }) 1... Kc6 { He steps aside. Move your king to e5 and take the space. [%cal Yd4e5] } 2. Ke5 { Your spare pawn step made him yield. Now watch your king walk all the way round his pawn while the c pawn waits. [%cal Gd4e5][%tail] } (2. Kd3 { That retreat gives up the win: stepping back to d3 lets him meet you move for move, and a tempo already spent cannot be spent twice. } 2... Kd5 3. Ke2 3... Kd6 { He restores the opposition. Use the space your pawn move created. [%cal Rd5d6] }) (2. Ke3 { This wins too: e3 keeps your king just as active, and the reserve tempo already did its job. } 2... Kd5 { Also winning. That pawn move is still in your pocket for the right moment. [%cal Bc6d5] }) (2. Ke4 { Also winning: e4 keeps your king central, and the reserve tempo already forced him off c6 for you. } 2... Kc7 { This also wins, and the spare tempo is still there to use later. [%cal Bc6c7] }) 2... Kb7 3. Kd5 3... Kb6 4. Kd6 4... Ka7 5. Kc5 5... Ka6 6. Kc6 6... Ka7 7. Kxb5 7... Kb7 8. Kc5 8... Kc7 9. b5 9... Kd8 10. b6 10... Kd7 11. c4 11... Ke6 12. b7 12... Ke5 13. b8=Q+ { A new queen on b8, with check, from one saved pawn step. [%csl Gb8] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: Spend the reserve pawn move"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3k4/8/3K4/2P5/8/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
