{ Your king is ahead. Can the spare pawn step make his king move? } 1. c6 { You spend the spare move. His king must give up the opposition. [%cal Gc5c6] } (1. Kc6 { Also a win: approaching with the king first keeps the pawn move in reserve, and nothing is lost by waiting. } 1... Ke7 { You still have the spare pawn move. This line just spends it right away. [%cal Bd8e7] }) 1... Kc8 { He steps aside to c8, since the pawn move you just spent took away every other square. } 2. c7 { Your pawn reaches the seventh under king protection. [%cal Gc6c7] } (2. Kd5 { This retreat only draws: stepping back to d5 gives up the shelter your king was giving the pawn, and his king slips in front of it. } 2... Kc7 3. Kc5 3... Kc8 { His king blocks the pawn. Keep your king in front and push the pawn. [%cal Rc7c8] }) 2... Kb7 { He moves away from the promotion square, since staying on c7 would run straight into your king. } 3. Kd7 { Your king on d7 stands right next to c8 and guards it, so the pawn is safe. Now watch it finish. [%cal Gd6d7][%tail] } (3. Ke6 { That gives away the pawn and the win: stepping away to e6 leaves c7 unguarded, and his king simply eats the pawn. } 3... Kxc7 { He takes your last pawn. Draw. Guard the pawn as well as its promotion square. [%cal Rb7c7] }) 3... Ka6 4. c8=Q+ { Your pawn is a queen on c8, and she arrives with check while his king stands out on a6. [%csl Gc8] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: A checking pawn sacrifice saves the draw"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/4kp2/8/5PP1/4K3/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Opponent"]
[Black "Student"]
