[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 69"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "draw"]

{ You are a pawn down. White will bring the king forward; look for a saving pawn move. } 1. Ke4 { White takes the opposition. Can your pawn interrupt the king invasion? } 1... f5+ { Your pawn checks and offers an exchange. [%cal Gf6f5] } (1... Kd6 { That loses: heading to d6 abandons the opposition next to the pawns, and White's king simply walks around you. } 2. Kf5 2... Ke7 3. Kg6 { This lets White outflank your king and walk a pawn home. Stay beside the pawns with the opposition. [%cal Rf5g6] }) (1... Ke7 { That loses too: e7 gives up contact with the pawns, and White's king slips past to escort one home. } 2. Kd5 2... Kd7 3. f5 { White's king slips around yours and escorts a pawn in. Hold the opposition next to the pawns instead. [%cal Rf4f5] }) 2. gxf5+ { White accepts the trade and checks your king: now only one pawn is left for each side to fight over. } 2... Kf6 { You take the opposition beside the remaining pawn. [%cal Ge6f6] } (2... Kd6 { This loses: d6 breaks contact with the last pawn, and White's king simply walks around to escort it. } 3. Kd4 3... Kc6 4. Ke5 { Losing contact here lets White outflank you and escort a pawn through. Keep the opposition by the pawns. [%cal Rd4e5] }) 3. Kd5 { White's king steps aside to d5, leaving the pawns on f5 and f4 completely unguarded. } 3... Kxf5 { You take the front pawn on f5, and the one left on f4 has nobody to guard it either. Watch it go. [%cal Gf6f5][%tail] } (3... Kf7 { That loses: Kf7 lets White's king step back in and shepherd the f pawn home, since you never collected it. } 4. Ke5 4... Ke8 5. f6 { White keeps the dangerous pawn. Your king needed to collect it immediately. [%cal Rf5f6] }) 4. Kd4 4... Kxf4 { Your king takes the last pawn on f4. Nothing is left but two bare kings, and the game is a draw. [%csl Gf4] } 0-1


[Event "Lasker endgames: Create a distant passed pawn"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/1pp1k2p/P5p1/1PP3P1/5K2/8/8_b_-_-_0_2"]
[White "Student"]
