[Block "Outside passed pawns"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 58"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Both kings will detour to grab an outside passed pawn before racing back to the real battle. Can you finish closer to the remaining pawns? } 6... Kf5 { Your king approaches the f pawn from the side nearest the queen side. [%cal Gf6f5] } (6... Kg6 { Also winning: g6 still lets you scoop up the f pawn on the way, losing nothing from the direct route. } 7. Kh3 { You still win the race back. Your direct route stays closer to the queen side. [%cal Bh4h3] }) 7. Kxh5 { White takes your distant h pawn. } 7... Kxf4 { You take his passer and finish nearer the remaining pawns. [%cal Gf5f4] } (7... Ke6 { That loses: turning away to e6 leaves the f pawn uncollected, and White's own king escorts it home instead. } 8. Kg5 8... Kd5 9. Kf6 { White can escort the f pawn. Collect that passer before going to the other wing. [%cal Rg5f6] }) 8. Kg6 { His king starts the long trip back to the queenside, a full move behind where yours is already heading. } 8... Ke5 { Your king turns for the queen side a full move ahead of his, and the pawns stay where they are. Now watch the race. [%cal Gf4e5][%tail] } (8... Ke3 { Still winning: e3 is just as quick a road back to the queenside, since White's king is no closer from g6. } 9. Kf7 { Also winning. Your king still wins the race back to the queen side. [%cal Bg6f7] }) (8... Ke4 { This wins too: e4 covers the same ground toward the queenside without losing a step. } 9. Kf6 { This wins too. You still beat him back to the queen side. [%cal Bg6f6] }) (8... Kf3 { Also winning: f3 reaches the queenside just as fast, since the head start is what matters, not the exact square. } 9. Kf7 { Still winning: your king still arrives on the queen side first. [%cal Bg6f7] }) (8... Kg4 { That loses the king race: g4 wanders away from the queenside instead of racing straight for it. } 9. Kf7 9... Kf5 10. Ke7 { White can reach the remaining pawns. Use your head start toward the queen side. [%cal Rf7e7] }) 9. Kg5 9... Kd4 10. Kf4 10... Kc3 11. Ke3 11... Kb2 12. Kd2 12... Kxa3 13. Kc3 13... Ka4 14. Kb2 14... Kxb4 { Both of his pawns are gone and yours on a6 and b5 have never moved. Only his bare king on b2 is left to chase them. [%csl Ga6,Gb5] } 0-1


[Event "Lasker endgames: Give a pawn to gain the opposition"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/8/p1k3p1/P5P1/1PK5/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
