[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 68"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ A pawn sacrifice can win opposition. Push your b pawn to b4 with check. [%cal Yb3b4] } 1. b4+ { Your sacrifice pulls his pawn off a5 and creates a useful king route. [%cal Gb3b4] } (1. Kb2 { Also winning: b2 keeps the pawn sacrifice in reserve while your king finds a better square first. } 1... Kd4 { Also winning. The b pawn lever is still there while your king improves. [%cal Bc5d4] }) (1. Kc2 { Also winning: c2 lets your king maneuver first, and the b pawn lever is still there whenever you want it. } 1... Kd5 { Your king can maneuver and still use the b pawn lever later. [%cal Bc5d5] }) (1. Kd2 { Also winning: d2 delays the sacrifice too, and nothing about the win depends on playing it right away. } 1... Kd4 { This wins too, and the b pawn break is still available once your king is better placed. [%cal Bc5d4] }) (1. Kd3 { This wins too: d3 keeps your king central, and the b pawn lever waits for whenever you choose to play it. } 1... Kd5 { Still winning: you can improve your king first and keep the b pawn lever in hand. [%cal Bc5d5] }) 1... axb4+ { He accepts with check. Move your king to b3 before taking the pawn. [%cal Yc3b3] } 2. Kb3 { Your king steps onto b3, ready to collect the pawn from a better square. [%cal Gc3b3] } (2. Kd3 { That gives away the win: d3 puts your king on the wrong side of the pawn, and Black's king reaches d6 to hold the opposition. } 2... Kd5 3. Kc2 3... Kd6 { Black can hold the opposition. Step to b3 before collecting the pawn. [%cal Rd5d6] }) 2... Kd6 { His king moves away. Take the pawn on b4 with your king. [%cal Yb3b4] } 3. Kxb4 { You take the pawn back on b4, and your passed a pawn has your king right beside it. Now watch it drag his king across. [%cal Gb3b4][%tail] } (3. a5 { Also winning: pushing a5 first leaves the loose b4 pawn for later, though grabbing it right away makes the win clearer. } 3... Ke5 { You can still win. Taking the loose pawn immediately makes the plan much clearer. [%cal Bd6e5] }) (3. Kc4 { Also winning: c4 keeps your king close enough to mop up the b4 pawn and escort the passer just the same. } 3... Ke5 { Also winning. Your king can still collect the b4 pawn and escort your passer. [%cal Bd6e5] }) 3... Kc6 4. Kc3 4... Kc5 5. Kd3 5... Kb4 6. Ke4 6... Kxa4 7. Kf5 7... Kb5 8. Kxg5 { He spent his king walking to a4 for your outside pawn, and while he did your king ate his last one. Only your pawn on g4 is left. [%csl Gg4] } 1-0


[Event "Lasker endgames: A rook for connected passed pawns"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2K5/3P3R/2k5/3r4/6p1/7p/8/8_b_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Opponent"]
[Black "Student"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0N4zuDFR/ljrxwth4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "2K5/3P3R/2k5/3r4/6p1/7p/8/8 b - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E13"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "Pawn sacrifices and breakthroughs"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Diagram 81"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Can a rook sacrifice stop his passer and set your own connected pawns free? } 1... Rxd7 { You remove the dangerous passer before starting your own pawn race. [%cal Gd5d7] } (1... g3 { That loses: pushing g3 first lets his rook check you before your pawns are organized enough to run. } 2. Rh6+ 2... Kc5 3. Rxh3 3... Kc4 4. Rxg3 4... Rxd7 5. Kxd7 { White wins material with rook checks. Remove the d pawn with your rook first. [%cal Rc8d7] }) 2. Rxd7 { He has to take with the rook: your king on c6 covers d7, so his own king could not go there. [%cal Gc6d7] } 2... g3 { Both passers stand on the third row now, and one rook cannot stop them both. [%cal Gg4g3] } (2... h2 { Also winning: starting with the h pawn works too, since the g pawn arrives just in time to guard it. } 3. Rh7 3... g3 { Your g pawn now guards h2. This move order also wins. [%cal Bg4g3,Gg3h2] }) 3. Rh7 { He swings his rook behind the h pawn to h7, ready to gobble it the moment it stands undefended. } 3... g2 { Your pawn on g2 is guarded by the one on h3, so the rook cannot simply eat it. [%cal Gg3g2] } (3... h2 { Also winning: this pawn steps up and the one on g3 guards it. } 4. Rxh2 4... gxh2 { Your pawn takes the rook and walks in on h1. Winning too, and just as quickly. [%cal Gg3h2] }) (3... Kd6 { That loses: wandering the king to d6 wastes a move the pawns needed to keep running. } 4. Rxh3 4... g2 5. Rg3 { His rook stops your last pawn from g3 and then his king comes. You are lost. [%csl Rg2] }) 4. Rg7 { He lines the rook up behind your pawn on g2: promote there and he takes the new queen. Grabbing on h3 instead would let it through. } 4... h2 { Both pawns are one step from queening and his rook can only watch one of the two squares. [%cal Gh3h2][%tail] } (4... g1=Q { Promoting at once, and his rook simply takes her. } 5. Rxg1 5... h2 6. Rh1 { Your queen is gone for nothing and the last pawn is stopped dead on h2. You are lost. [%csl Rh2] }) (4... Kd5 { Also winning: your king strolls closer while the pawns sit tight. } 5. Rxg2 5... hxg2 { Your h pawn takes the rook and heads for g1. Winning too, and his rook is gone. [%cal Gh3g2] }) (4... Kd6 { Winning as well: the king steps up and leaves the pawns to it. } 5. Rg3 { You still win, because his rook cannot hold the g column and the h pawn at once. [%cal Bh3h2] }) (4... Kc5 { This wins too: walking the king down covers the same ground, and the pawns run just as well without it. } 5. Kc7 { The win holds: his king starts back, but your pawns are already too far along. [%cal Bh3h2] }) (4... Kb6 { Winning too: heading off to the queen side costs nothing, since the pawns queen on their own either way. } 5. Rg6+ { A check only nudges your king along, and both pawns are still where you left them. [%cal Bb6c5] }) (4... Kb5 { Also winning: the king goes wandering while the pawns wait. } 5. Rg5+ { His checks run out soon, and the pawns are each one step from queening. [%cal Bb5c4] }) 5. Rxg2 5... h1=Q { A new queen on h1, and the rook on g2 cannot reach her. A rook for two pawns, and the two pawns won. [%csl Gh1] } 0-1


[Event "Lasker endgames: Deflect the king to create connected passers"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/7p/1Pk5/5p2/3PpKP1/8/8/8_b_-_-_0_10"]
[White "Opponent"]
[Black "Student"]
