[Block "Pawn sacrifices and breakthroughs"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Part I, Chapter V, Bird-Janowski, Diagram 87"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ The rooks are gone. Can a pawn sacrifice pull his king away and free your king side? } 10... e3 { Your pawn sacrifice deflects his king and opens a capture on g4. [%cal Ge4e3] } (10... h5 { That loses: pushing h5 first skips the sacrifice on e3, so White simply takes on h5 and stays fully defended. } 11. gxh5 { Taking h5 removes your pawn partner; taking f5 also wins for White. [%cal Rg4h5] }) 11. Kxe3 { He removes the more advanced pawn on e3, since ignoring it would let your e pawn queen in two moves flat. } 11... fxg4 { You remove his g pawn and create connected passed pawns. [%cal Gf5g4] } (11... h5 { That loses: playing h5 before capturing on g4 lets White's own g pawn simply take it. } 12. gxh5 { He removes the h pawn instead of taking f5. Your connected-pawn plan is gone. [%cal Rg4h5] }) 12. Kf4 { His king hurries back to the kingside, since the connected pawns you are about to make are the real threat now. } 12... h5 { Your pawns on g4 and h5 guard each other, so his king on f4 cannot touch either one. Connected passers, bought with one pawn. [%cal Gh7h5][%csl Gg4] } (12... g3 { That only draws: pushing g3 alone runs the pawn ahead of its partner, and his king simply scoops it up. } 13. Kxg3 { His king takes the pawn. Advance its neighbor first to keep the win. [%cal Rf4g3] }) 0-1


[Event "Lasker endgames: Give away the rook for stalemate"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2rk4/8/2PK4/8/8/8/2R5/8_b_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Opponent"]
[Black "Student"]
