{ You are a pawn down, and his king is closing in: chase the stalemate. Your king has no legal move and is not in check, so the game is drawn. Send your rook to c7. [%cal Yc8c7] } 1... Rc7 { You keep the rook ready to check along the seventh rank. [%cal Gc8c7] } (1... Rxc6+ { That loses: taking on c6 wins nothing but a rook trade, and White simply keeps his extra pawn. } 2. Rxc6 { White wins your rook, without a stalemate. Keep the rook ready for the d7 sacrifice. [%cal Rc2c6] }) 2. Rg2 { White brings the rook over. Put your rook on d7 with check and offer it to the pawn. [%cal Yc7d7] } 2... Rd7+ { Check! His king cannot take on d7, since your own king on d8 guards that square, so only his pawn can, and then your king has no legal move anywhere. Stalemate. The draw is yours. [%csl Gd8] } (2... Kc8 { That loses immediately: your king walks into a mating net. } 3. Rg8# { Mate! His rook seals the back row, and his pawn on c6 covers the squares beside your king. [%cal Rg2g8] }) 0-1


[Event "Lasker endgames: Leave the bishop pawn undefended"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/1K6/2P5/2q5/8/3k4/8/8/8_b_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
