{ You are a pawn down, but a rook-pawn fortress can hold the draw. Move your king to c4 and attack the pawn. [%cal Yd3c4] } 1... Kc4 { You attack the pawn instead of chasing its king. [%cal Gd3c4] } (1... Ke3 { That loses: e3 abandons the rook column, and his king slips out to b5, freeing the pawn to run. } 2. Kb5 2... Kd4 3. a4 { This lets White's king off the rook column to escort the pawn through. Keep that exit shut. [%cal Ra2a4] }) 2. a4 { The pawn advances. Move your king to c5 to keep the exit closed. [%cal Yc4c5] } 2... Kc5 { You keep the king on the rook column. [%cal Gc4c5] } (2... Kd4 { That loses too: stepping to d4 opens the exit just the same, and his king slips out to escort the pawn home. } 3. Kb5 3... Ke5 4. Kc5 { White slips off the rook column and walks the pawn home. Hold the exit closed instead. [%cal Rb5c5] }) 3. a5 { The pawn advances again. Move your king to c6 for the opposition. [%cal Yc5c6] } 3... Kc6 { You hold his king on the a column. Now shadow him up that column and watch what happens. [%cal Gc5c6][%tail] } (3... Kd5 { This loses: d5 opens the exit one square early, and his king slips out to b6 all the same. } 4. Kb6 4... Kd6 5. a6 { Letting him off the rook column costs the fortress. Keep the exit closed. [%cal Ra5a6] }) 4. Ka7 4... Kc7 5. a6 5... Kc8 6. Kb6 6... Kb8 7. a7+ 7... Ka8 { Your king sits in the corner on a8, right in front of the pawn on a7. His king must either stalemate you or let the pawn go. Drawn. [%csl Ga8] } 0-1


