{ You are the one defending, and his passed pawn is a step from queening. Can you find the fortress corner his bishop can never cover, and hold the draw? } 3. Nxf2 { You give the knight to remove the dangerous f pawn. [%cal Ge4f2] } (3. Nd6 { That loses: retreating the knight leaves the f pawn free to queen next move. } 3... f1=Q+ 4. Ke4 { Black promotes. Remove the f pawn before heading for the fortress. [%cal Rd3e4] }) 3... Kxf2 { He recaptures with the king. Taking with the bishop instead would leave the same wrong-colored bishop. } 4. Kd2 { Your king sets off for h1, the one corner his dark-squared bishop can never cover. Now watch him chase you all the way there. [%cal Gd3d2][%csl Gh1][%tail] } (4. Kc2 { Also a draw: c2 heads for the same corner, and no square the bishop stands on ever controls that light corner. } 4... Ba5 { Also draws. Your king still reaches h1, a corner the bishop cannot cover. [%cal Bb6a5] }) (4. Kc3 { Also a draw: c3 is just as good a road to h1, since no bishop move changes which corner is safe. } 4... Kf3 { This holds too. The bishop cannot control h1, and your king still gets there. [%cal Bf2f3][%csl Gh1] }) (4. Kc4 { Also a draw: c4 costs a little time, but the bishop can never reach h1 no matter how long the walk. } 4... Ke2 { Still a draw: h1 is out of the bishop's reach, and your king arrives in time. [%cal Bf2e2] }) (4. Ke4 { Also a draw: stepping toward e4 first still leaves enough time to turn back for h1. } 4... Kg3 { This route holds too. Your retreat makes the route to the safe corner easier to see. [%cal Bf2g3] }) 4... Kg3 5. Ke1 5... Kg4 6. Kf1 6... Kxh5 7. Kg2 7... Kg5 8. Kh1 { Your king is home on h1. He won the h pawn for nothing: his bishop on b6 is dark, that corner is light, and nothing can drive you out. Drawn. [%csl Gh1][%cal Rb6g1] } 1-0
